</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Probing Configuration</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each of our supported models, we extract the hidden representations of the middle (e.g., 16) and last (e.g., 32) of the <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">post-attention layer norm</span> module (or equivalent) using our library. This is because we require a 4096-dimensional (or equivalent) representation that can be efficiently stored and contains some relevant information about the input.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each probe is instantiated with <span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ReLU</span> activation and a hidden size of 512, selected empirically to balance performance and efficiency. The input and output sizes are determined dynamically based on the tensors and labels in the input database. We tune the hyperparameters, <span id="A2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">learning_rate</span>, <span id="A2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">num_epochs</span> and <span id="A2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">batch_size</span>, to find the best combinations that incurs the lowest mean validation loss after <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-fold cross-validation with <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math>. With 3 options for each former parameter, we search through <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{3}=27" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{3}=27</annotation></semantics></math> configurations. Lastly, using the best training configuration, we train two new models from scratch on the original and shuffled datasets, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
